-- moot.rofl — MOOT: proving which feature flags are dead.
--
-- A flag IS a rule-as-data. Its enabling condition is a predicate over a
-- context (segment, region, version, bucket, channel, another flag), and a
-- config file is already the reflection of that rule — nothing needs
-- translating. So these rules are siblings of boot.rofl's `malformed`,
-- `breach`, `leak`, `forged`, `unmoded` and `undefined_premise`: audits over
-- a reflection, in `[audit]`, each with a derivation tree.
--
-- THE DECIDABILITY BOUNDARY, STATED ONCE, HERE.
-- Every condition constrains ONE dimension of the context, every dimension is
-- FINITE and declared as data (`dim/1`, `dom/2`), and clauses are conjunctions
-- of such conditions. Two consequences carry this whole file:
--
--   (1) the set of contexts a clause admits is the PRODUCT of its
--       per-dimension admitted sets. So satisfiability decomposes: a clause is
--       satisfiable iff no single dimension of it is empty;
--   (2) containment decomposes too. For a NON-EMPTY clause C2,
--       contexts(C2) subset-of contexts(C1)  iff  for every dimension D,
--       admits(C2,D) subset-of admits(C1,D). (Left to right: extend any value
--       of C2 on D to a full context of C2, which must then be a context of
--       C1. Right to left: componentwise.)
--
-- (2) is why `shadowed` is exact here and not an approximation, and the
-- non-emptiness side condition is not decoration: the empty set is contained
-- in everything, while its per-dimension image is not. `contains/2` carries
-- that guard explicitly.
--
-- The boundary is the hypothesis, not the operators. Comparisons are fine: on
-- a finite domain `version >= 7` denotes a finite set and is enumerated below
-- by an ordinary rule. What breaks the decomposition is an UNBOUNDED domain
-- (a timestamp, a real number), or a constraint RELATING two dimensions
-- (`bucket < version * 8`), because then contexts(C) is no longer a product
-- and (1) and (2) both fail. That is where SMT starts. README.md says so
-- again, with the measured sample size of the enumeration oracle.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- the input schema: everything the config parser emits.
--
-- Declared, not merely populated. boot.rofl's `undefined_premise` audit reads
-- an unpopulated positive premise as a misspelling, and it is right to: a rule
-- pack loaded without its host facts is mute. Declaring the inputs is the
-- remedy, and it doubles as the schema a reader needs.

edb(flag).        -- flag(F).            a flag exists
edb(ordered).     -- ordered(F, C, I).   flag F has clause C at position I
edb(cond_of).     -- cond_of(C, L).      clause C carries condition L
edb(cond).        -- cond(L, D, Op, K).  L constrains dimension D: Op K
edb(cond_alt).    -- cond_alt(L, V).     a member of L's value set (oneof/noneof)
edb(requires).    -- requires(C, G).     clause C needs flag G to be on
edb(req_at).      -- req_at(C, K, G).    C's K-th requirement, 1-based
edb(req_count).   -- req_count(C, N).    how many requirements C has
edb(dim).         -- dim(D).             a context dimension
edb(dom).         -- dom(D, V).          V is a value of dimension D
edb(exclusive).   -- exclusive(F, G).    F and G are declared mutually exclusive
edb(ctx).         -- ctx(D, V).          the ONE context under evaluation

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- layer 1 — what a single condition admits.
--
-- The operator semantics live here, in rules, not in the parser. The host
-- turns config text into `cond/4` and `cond_alt/2` facts and stops; whether
-- `version >= 7` admits 6 is inferred. `dom(D, V)` on every rule is what keeps
-- the answer inside the declared finite domain: a condition naming a value the
-- domain does not have admits nothing, which is the correct reading of a typo.

cond_admits(L, V) :- cond(L, D, eq, K), dom(D, V), V = K.
cond_admits(L, V) :- cond(L, D, ne, K), dom(D, V), V != K.
cond_admits(L, V) :- cond(L, D, ge, K), dom(D, V), V >= K.
cond_admits(L, V) :- cond(L, D, le, K), dom(D, V), V <= K.
cond_admits(L, V) :- cond(L, D, oneof, _), dom(D, V), cond_alt(L, V).
cond_admits(L, V) :- cond(L, D, noneof, _), dom(D, V), not cond_alt(L, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- layer 2 — what a CLAUSE admits, one dimension at a time.
--
-- The intersection over the clause's conditions on that dimension, written as
-- a double negation because a conjunction over an open set of conditions is a
-- universal. A dimension no condition of C mentions is admitted whole, which
-- falls out rather than being special-cased: nothing rejects it.

clause_known(C)  :- ordered(_, C, _).
rejects(C, D, V) :- cond_of(C, L), cond(L, D, _, _), dom(D, V), not cond_admits(L, V).
admits(C, D, V)  :- clause_known(C), dom(D, V), not rejects(C, D, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- layer 3 — emptiness. By (1), one empty dimension kills the whole clause.

-- `empty` is asked only where the clause rejects SOMETHING on that dimension:
-- a dimension no condition of C mentions is admitted whole and cannot be
-- empty. The second rule is the degenerate case that guard would otherwise
-- lose — a dimension declared with no values at all, which makes every clause
-- empty on it. (Written the direct way, over `clause_known(C), dim(D)`, this
-- rule scanned `nonempty` once per (clause, dimension) pair: 21M unifications
-- on the self-application of section 7, against 750k here.)
nonempty(C, D)   :- admits(C, D, V).
dom_known(D)     :- dom(D, _).
empty(C, D)      :- rejects(C, D, _), not nonempty(C, D).
empty(C, D)      :- clause_known(C), dim(D), not dom_known(D).
dead_clause(C)   :- empty(C, D).

-- The human-readable naming of an emptiness: a PAIR of conditions on the same
-- dimension that share no value. Sufficient, NOT necessary — three conditions
-- can be pairwise compatible and jointly empty, which is what `loyalty_banner`
-- does in the demo config. `empty/2` above is the proof; `conflict/4` is the
-- sentence, and when there is no such pair the report says the emptiness is
-- three-way rather than inventing one. Restricted to already-empty dimensions
-- because that is the only place anybody reads it.

share(L1, L2)          :- cond_of(C, L1), cond_of(C, L2), cond(L1, D, _, _),
                          cond(L2, D, _, _), cond_admits(L1, V), cond_admits(L2, V).
conflict(C, D, L1, L2) :- empty(C, D), cond_of(C, L1), cond_of(C, L2),
                          cond(L1, D, _, _), cond(L2, D, _, _), L1 != L2,
                          not share(L1, L2).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- layer 4 — liveness through flag requirements.
--
-- "Every requirement of C is live" is a universal over a set, and the obvious
-- spelling (`blocked(C) :- requires(C,G), not live(G)`) puts `live` and
-- `blocked` in a NEGATIVE CYCLE, which boot.rofl rejects as unstratifiable and
-- is right to. Indexing the requirements and walking them turns the universal
-- into a positive recursion: `ok_from(C,K)` means "requirements K..N of C are
-- all live", the base case is the position past the last one, and the only
-- negation left in this layer is `not dead_clause`, which sits below.
--
-- This is a real cost of relation-level stratification, paid deliberately and
-- once. It is also the reason `live/1` recurses positively and therefore
-- carries a counting annotation that means something (see README).

ok_from(C, N1)   :- req_count(C, N), N1 is N + 1.
ok_from(C, K)    :- req_at(C, K, G), live(G), K1 is K + 1, ok_from(C, K1).
usable(C)        :- clause_known(C), not dead_clause(C), ok_from(C, 1).
live(F)          :- ordered(F, C, _), usable(C).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE FIVE VERDICTS. All in [audit], all with a derivation tree.
-- ===========================================================================

-- 1. UNREACHABLE — no context whatsoever turns this flag on. Not "we saw no
-- traffic": there is no traffic to see. `whynot live(F)` is the demonstration,
-- and it recurses through the requirement chain on its own.

unreachable[audit](F) :- flag(F), not live(F).

-- 2. SHADOWED — another clause of the same flag admits every context this one
-- admits, so deleting this clause changes no decision anywhere. This is
-- entailment between rule bodies, not derivation, and by (2) it is decided
-- exactly: `escapes` looks for a single (dimension, value) witness that C2
-- admits and C1 does not, and its absence IS the containment.
--
-- Three guards, each load-bearing:
--   * `not dead_clause(C2)` — the empty set is contained in everything but its
--     per-dimension image is not, so (2) only holds for a non-empty C2. A dead
--     clause is unreachable, not shadowed;
--   * `req_escape` — containment of contexts is not enough. If C1 needs a flag
--     C2 does not, C1 can fail where C2 fires. Every requirement of C1 must
--     also be one of C2's;
--   * `I < J` in `sibling` — the earlier clause is the survivor, so exactly one
--     of a mutually-containing pair is condemned, deterministically.

-- `escapes` is written positively for the same reason `narrows` is: on a value
-- of dom(D), "C1 does not admit it" IS "C1 rejects it", by the admits rule
-- above, and `rejects` is one fact per (condition, excluded value) where
-- `admits` is one per (clause, dimension, value). Same relation, a fiftieth of
-- the scanning: on the self-application of section 7 this rule alone was 25M
-- unifications and is now under half a million.
sibling(C1, C2)       :- ordered(F, C1, I), ordered(F, C2, J), I < J.
escapes(C2, C1, D, V) :- sibling(C1, C2), rejects(C1, D, V), admits(C2, D, V).
req_escape(C2, C1)    :- sibling(C1, C2), requires(C1, G), not requires(C2, G).
contains(C1, C2)      :- sibling(C1, C2), not dead_clause(C2),
                         not escapes(C2, C1, _, _), not req_escape(C2, C1).

shadowed[audit](F, C2, C1) :- ordered(F, C2, _), sibling(C1, C2), contains(C1, C2).

-- 3. TAUTOLOGICAL — a clause that narrows nothing on any dimension and needs
-- no flag. The flag is effectively deleted; only the branch in the code is
-- left, and every reader of that branch still believes it is conditional.
--
-- The claim is about ONE clause. A flag whose clauses are individually partial
-- but jointly cover the space is also always on, and this rule does not say
-- so: DNF tautology does not decompose per dimension the way (1) and (2) do.
-- The enumeration oracle in demo.ts measures exactly that gap and the report
-- names the flags it finds there. Stated, not silently approximated.

-- `narrows` is the positive spelling of "some value of D this clause does not
-- admit", and it is `rejects` verbatim: by the rule above, a value of dom(D) is
-- admitted exactly when nothing rejects it. Written the obvious way — over
-- `not admits` — this rule costs a scan of `admits` per (clause, value) pair
-- and dominated the whole run at 5.5M of them. Same relation, same verdicts,
-- one join instead.
narrows(C, D)   :- rejects(C, D, _).
total_clause(C) :- clause_known(C), not narrows(C, _).

tautological[audit](F, C) :- ordered(F, C, _), total_clause(C), req_count(C, 0).

-- 4. CONTRADICTORY — two flags the config declares mutually exclusive, whose
-- clauses admit a common context. On that overlap BOTH are on, so which branch
-- of the code runs is decided by the order the branches are written in, not by
-- any condition anybody wrote. By (1) the overlap is non-empty iff the two
-- clauses meet on EVERY dimension, which is again a per-dimension question.
--
-- `usable` on both sides keeps the verdict about reachable behaviour; a
-- witness context is then built and CHECKED against `flag_on/1` below, so the
-- overlap is not merely counted, it is exhibited.

rival(C1, C2)      :- exclusive(F, G), ordered(F, C1, _), ordered(G, C2, _).
meets(C1, C2, D)   :- rival(C1, C2), admits(C1, D, V), admits(C2, D, V).
misses(C1, C2, D)  :- rival(C1, C2), dim(D), not meets(C1, C2, D).
compatible(C1, C2) :- rival(C1, C2), usable(C1), usable(C2), not misses(C1, C2, _).

contradictory[audit](F, G, C1, C2) :- exclusive(F, G), ordered(F, C1, _),
                                      ordered(G, C2, _), compatible(C1, C2).

-- 5. DEPENDENT — every usable clause of F requires G, so F's own conditions
-- never decide anything on their own: F is on only where G is. Deleting G
-- kills F, and the segment/region/version predicates on F are theatre.
-- `live(F)` keeps it separate from `unreachable`: a flag with no usable clause
-- satisfies the universal vacuously and has a better verdict already.

gated(F, G)   :- ordered(F, C, _), requires(C, G).
free_of(F, G) :- gated(F, G), ordered(F, C, _), usable(C), not requires(C, G).

dependent[audit](F, G) :- gated(F, G), live(F), not free_of(F, G).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- the evaluator: ONE context, asserted by the host, and what it turns on.
--
-- This is what makes best-derivation an answer rather than a claim. The host
-- reads `admits/3` to build a candidate enabling context, asserts it as
-- `ctx/2`, and the engine derives `flag_on/1` with a proof that bottoms out in
-- the same `admits/3` facts the audits used. A context nobody can verify is a
-- guess; this one comes back with a tree.
--
-- `ctx_bad` over `dim/1` rather than over the asserted values is deliberate:
-- with no context asserted, ctx_dim_ok is empty, every clause is bad, and
-- nothing is on. A missing input reads as "unknown", never as "everything".

ctx_dim_ok(C, D) :- ctx(D, V), admits(C, D, V).
ctx_bad(C)       :- clause_known(C), dim(D), not ctx_dim_ok(C, D).
ctx_ok(C)        :- clause_known(C), not ctx_bad(C).

on_here(C, N1)   :- req_count(C, N), N1 is N + 1.
on_here(C, K)    :- req_at(C, K, G), flag_on(G), K1 is K + 1, on_here(C, K1).
flag_on(F)       :- ordered(F, C, _), ctx_ok(C), on_here(C, 1).

-- Required results on a healthy config, all checked in test/example-moot.test.ts:
--   ? unstratified(X)            -> empty   (boot.rofl computes stratum/2 over these)
--   ? undefined_premise[audit](R, Rel) -> empty
--   ? leak[audit](A, B)          -> empty   (the [audit] heads declare their bridge)
--   whynot live(f_new_checkout)  -> both clauses, two different causes
--   why flag_on(f_...)           -> a context, proved, ready to paste into a test
